1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,640
I've worked with many different teams on building agentic workflows and I've found that one of the biggest predictors for whether someone is able to do it really well versus be less efficient at it is whether or not they're able to drive a really disciplined evaluation process.

2
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:25,420
So your ability to drive evals for your agentic workflow makes a huge difference in your ability to build them effectively.

3
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:35,280
In this video, we'll take a quick overview of how to build evals and this is a subject that we'll actually go into much deeper in a later module in this course.

4
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:36,260
Let's take a look.

5
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:48,380
After building an agentic workflow like this one for responding to customer order inquiries, it turns out that it's very difficult to know in advance what are the things that could go wrong.

6
00:00:48,380 --> 00:00:59,860
And so rather than trying to build evaluations in advance, what I recommend is you just look at the outputs and manually look for things that you wish it was doing better.

7
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:07,540
For example, maybe you read a lot of outputs and find that it is unexpectedly mentioning your competitors more than it should.

8
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:13,520
Maybe businesses don't want their agents to mention competitors because it's just an awkward situation.

9
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:20,640
And if you read some of these outputs, maybe you'll find that it sometimes says, I'm glad you shocked with us. We're much better than a competitor compco.

10
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:23,180
Or maybe sometimes say, sure, it should be fun.

11
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:25,500
Unlike rivalco, we make returns easy.

12
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:30,380
And you may look at this and go, gee, I really don't want this to mention competitors.

13
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:37,040
This would be the example of a problem that is really hard to anticipate in advance of building this agentic workflow.

14
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:50,920
So the best practice is really to build it first and then examine it to figure out where it is not yet satisfactory and then to find ways to evaluate as well as improve the system to eliminate the ways that it is still not yet satisfactory.

15
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:57,980
Assuming your business considers it an error or mistake to mention competitors in this way.

16
00:01:57,980 --> 00:02:08,620
Then as you work on eliminating these competitor mentions, one way to track progress is to add an evaluation or an eval to track how often this error occurs.

17
00:02:08,940 --> 00:02:15,440
So if you have a named list of competitors like compco, rivalco, the otherco, then you can actually write code.

18
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:25,640
So just search in your own output for how often it mentions these competitors by name and count up as a number, as a fraction of the overall responses.

19
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:29,160
how frequently it mistakenly mentions competitors.

20
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:37,900
One nice thing about the problem of competitor mentions is an objective metric, meaning either the competitor was mentioned or not.

21
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:45,640
And for objective criteria, you can write code to check for how often this specific error occurs.

22
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:54,540
But because elements output free text, there are also going to be criteria by which you want to evaluate this output that may be more subjective.

23
00:02:54,540 --> 00:02:59,060
And where it's harder to just write code to output a black and white score.

24
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:05,560
In this case, using a LLM as a judge is a common technique to evaluate the output.

25
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:15,040
So for example, if you're building a research agent to do research on different topics, then you can use another LLM and prompt it to maybe say,

26
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:21,780
assign the following essay quality score between one and five, where one is the worst and five is the best essay.

27
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:27,520
Here I'm using a Python expression to mean copy paste the generated essay into this.

28
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:32,480
So you can prompt the LLM to read the essay and assign it a quality score.

29
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:36,900
Then I might ask the research agent to write a number of different research reports.

30
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:42,860
For example, on recent developments in black hole science, or using robots to harvest fruit.

31
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:49,440
And then in this example, maybe the judge LLM assigns the essay on black holes a score of three.

32
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:52,200
The essay on robot harvesting a score of four.

33
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:57,600
And as you work on improving your research agent, hopefully you see these scores go up over time.

34
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:03,880
It turns out, by the way, that LLMs are actually not that good at these one to five scale ratings.

35
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:07,880
You can give it a shot, but I personally tend not to use this technique that much myself.

36
00:04:08,140 --> 00:04:13,980
But in the later modules, you learn some better techniques to have an LLM output more accurate scores

37
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:16,460
than R scale to output scores on the one to five scale.

38
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:22,040
Although some people will do this maybe at an initial cut as an LLM as judge type of eval.

39
00:04:22,320 --> 00:04:27,580
Just to give a preview of some of the agentic AI evals that you'll learn about later in this course,

40
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:32,640
You've already heard me talk about how you can write codes to evaluate objective criteria,

41
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:37,540
such as did it mention the competitor or not, or use an LLM as a judge for more subjective criteria,

42
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:39,380
such as what's the quality of this essay.

43
00:04:39,580 --> 00:04:42,220
But later, you learn about two major types of evals.

44
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:46,620
One is end-to-end, where you measure the output quality of the entire agent,

45
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:53,760
as well as component level evals, where you might measure the quality of the output of a single step in the agentic workflow.

46
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:58,220
And it turns out that these are useful for driving different parts of the development process.

47
00:04:58,540 --> 00:05:02,940
And one thing I do a lot as well is just examine the intermediate outputs,

48
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:05,840
or sometimes you call these the traces of the LLM,

49
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,940
in order to understand where it is falling short of my expectations.

50
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:15,200
And we call this error analysis, where we just read through the intermediate outputs every single step

51
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:17,420
to try to spot opportunities for improvement.

52
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:22,200
And it turns out being able to do evals and error analysis is a really key skill,

53
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:26,720
so we have much more to say about this in the fourth module in this course.

54
